-- Q817: Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): SQL injection inserts malicious SQL statements into input fields that the application passes directly to the database without proper validation or parameterization. Under NIST SP 800-95 Section 4, this enables unauthorized data extraction, modification, or deletion from the backend database.
Wrong (B): Cross-site scripting injects malicious client-side scripts into web pages rendered for other users rather than executing database commands on the server.
Wrong (C): Buffer overflow exploits memory allocation flaws by writing data beyond allocated boundaries, targeting application memory rather than database query logic.
Wrong (D): Session hijacking involves stealing or forging session tokens to impersonate authenticated users, not injecting commands into database input fields.$EXPL$
